                <pb facs="0521" n="409" sameAs="frus:frus1910/pg409"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">File No. 24354/2.</note>
                <head><hi rendition="#italic">Chargé Monroe to the Secretary of State.</hi></head>
                <opener><seg rendition="#center">[Extract]</seg></opener>
                <opener><dateline rendition="#float-right"><placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                                >American Legation</hi>,<lb/><hi rendition="#italic">San
                            Jose</hi></placeName>, <date calendar="gregorian" when="1910-04-16"><hi
                                rendition="#italic">April 16, 1910</hi></date>.</dateline>
                    <seg rendition="#float-left">No. 1483.]</seg></opener>
                <p><hi rendition="#small-caps">Sir</hi>: I have the honor to report that since
                    midnight of April 12 a series of earthquake shocks have been felt here. One of
                    them is said to have been the strongest felt since 1888. There has been damage
                    to property, but no lives have been lost. The excitement was intense and
                    continues; parks and open places are filled with tents where people sleep at
                    night. Port Limon on the Atlantic and Puntarenas on the Pacific report no
                    shocks.</p>
                <p>I have, etc.,</p>
                <closer><signed><hi rendition="#small-caps">G. L. Monroe</hi>, Jr.</signed></closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
